module bindings.python.archive_member_write_member_filter;

// zipfile and tarfile both hand the extracting code the member names the
// archive itself declares: infolist/namelist on a ZipFile, getmembers/getnames
// on a TarFile. zipfile's own extract and extractall sanitise a member name
// before joining it to the destination (drive letters and absolute paths are
// dropped, `..` and `.` components removed), which is why
// pythonTarfileExtractWithoutMemberConstraint anchors on tarfile and leaves
// zipfile's convenience extraction alone -- and why a loop that only routes
// each entry through ZipFile.extract reports nothing here either. The manual
// loop is the other discipline: it takes the raw member name, joins it to a
// destination and opens a file at the joined path for writing. os.path.join
// adopts an absolute second argument unchanged and follows `..` components out
// of the destination, so the archive decides where the entry lands with the
// writing process's privileges (CWE-22, and CWE-59 where a link-shaped entry
// is also written through). A function that walks the member list and opens a
// destination file per entry, while naming none of the documented constraints
// on where an entry may land, is an extraction that cannot keep its own
// destination root.
//
// The write clause is the per-entry assembly rather than any directory
// creation: makedirs and mkdir also prepare the destination or build test
// fixtures beside a member walk that never extracts, an open/write pair alone
// matches a read-only walk that logs to stderr or dumps a stream into a
// scratch file, and an inline os.path.join matches a test that writes fixtures
// and only reads the member list back to assert on it. What the binding
// requires is the assembled destination -- an assignment whose right side is
// an os.path.join, the idiom a per-entry write loop uses to build the path it
// opens -- plus the open() and the write or copyfileobj on it. A loop that
// joins inline inside the open() call reports nothing; that is the residual
// recall cost of keeping member reads and fixture writes out. Every
// constraint read here is the API's own
// surface, in the register the tarfile binding established: a `..` component
// test, the containment normalisers (Path.resolve, relative_to,
// is_relative_to, os.path.abspath, os.path.realpath, os.path.isabs) and
// os.path.basename's single-component strip, tarfile's `filter=` keyword and
// data_filter/tar_filter, and an entry-type inspection (issym, islnk,
// linkname on a tar member; external_attr and flag_bits on a ZipInfo, the
// documented attributes carrying the unix mode word and the encrypted bit).
// The common read-only member walk -- namelist plus ZipFile.open(name) into
// memory -- names no write and reports nothing.
binding pythonArchiveMemberWriteWithoutMemberConstraint {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "python" and containsAny(node.context.call, ["infolist", "namelist", "getmembers", "getnames"]) and node.context.assign contains "=os.path.join(" and containsAny(node.context.call, ["write", "copyfileobj"]) and containsAny(node.context.call, ["open"]) and not (containsAny(node.context.call, ["resolve", "relative_to", "is_relative_to", "abspath", "realpath", "isabs", "basename"])) and not (node.context.expr contains "..") and not (node.context.identifier == "filter") and not (containsAny(node.context.selector, ["tarfile.data_filter", "tarfile.tar_filter"])) and not (containsAny(node.context.identifier, ["issym", "islnk", "linkname", "external_attr", "flag_bits"]))
  emit issue code.ArchiveSymlinkFilterBypass at node
}
